# Why do platforms win so big?
voices: {"a":"gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy","b":"gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow"}
prompt: "What are network effects? Why do these markets tip to one winner?"

## Two systems, one pocket
use: bar_chart
with:
    labels:
      - Android
      - iOS
      - All others
    values:
      - 72.36
      - 27.61
      - 0.03
    value_labels: false
    bar_colors: same
    icons:
      - robot
      - green apple
      - mobile phone
    ylim:
      - 0
      - 90
    y_label: Share of the world's smartphones, Oct 2026
    gap: 0.5
    label_size: 24
    box:
      x: 30
      'y': 150
      w: 600
      h: 510
sources:
    - id: statcounter
      title: Mobile Operating System Market Share Worldwide, October 2026
      authors: StatCounter Global Stats
      year: 2026
      finding: Android 72.36%, iOS 27.61%; every other mobile operating system together about 0.03% of page views.
      url: https://gs.statcounter.com/os-market-share/mobile/worldwide
    - id: wp_end
      title: Microsoft ends support for Windows 10 Mobile; BlackBerry ends legacy BB10 services
      authors: Microsoft; BlackBerry (carrier notices)
      year: 2022
      finding: Windows 10 Mobile support ended 10 December 2019; BlackBerry 10 services ended 4 January 2022.
    - id: rochet2003
      title: Platform Competition in Two-Sided Markets
      authors: Jean-Charles Rochet and Jean Tirole
      year: 2003
      finding: 'Journal of the European Economic Association 1(4): platforms court two groups whose benefits depend on each other''s participation.'
    - id: parker2005
      title: 'Two-Sided Network Effects: A Theory of Information Product Design'
      authors: Geoffrey G. Parker and Marshall W. Van Alstyne
      year: 2005
      finding: 'Management Science 51(10): 1494–1504; models network effects that run between two distinct markets.'
      doi: 10.1287/mnsc.1050.0400

