    text t3 "0.03%" #b5482e x 533 y 290 font_size 26 hidden true

    card title "Two systems, one pocket"

Why do platforms win so big? Start with the phone in your pocket: it almost certainly runs one of just two systems.
    draw axes

B: Android runs ab (@draw bar_1 t1@)out seventy-two percent of the world's smartphones, and App (@draw bar_2 t2@)le's iOS nearly all the rest. That's StatCounter's count for October 2026.

    ask "Drag the last bar to the share left for every other system put together." intro "Your guess." on bar_3 store g default 10 voice b
        right: About 0.03%: spot on.
        wrong: You said {g}%; it's 0.03%.

About three hundredths  (@draw t3 two@)of a percent. Practically nothing.

    erase two

B: And not for want of trying. Microsoft (@draw gone@) ended its Windows phones in 2019, and BlackBerry switched off its own system in 2022.

    erase gone

The obvious answe (@draw naive@)r: the winners simply made better phones.

B: Partl (@erase naive axes bar_1 bar_2 bar_3 t1 t2 t3@)y. But a phone is only as good as its (@draw users users_l devs devs_l@) apps, and app makers write for the phones people already own.

That's a network eff (@draw arr_top arr_top_l@)ect: a product is worth more the more peo (@highlight users effect pulse@)ple use it. Here it runs through a secon (@draw arr_bot arr_bot_l@)d group: more users draw more app makers, and more apps draw more users (@draw cross@). A cross-side network effect.

B: A business  (@draw platform@)in the middle of such a loop is a two-sided platform, like Harbourtown's fish-market app. Jean-Char (@draw who@)les Rochet and Jean Tirole, and Geoffrey Parker and Marshall Van A (@highlight arr_top arr_bot effect pulse@)lstyne, built its theory in the early 2000s.

    pause 0.4

## The tipping point
