    card title "The tipping point"

B: A loop be (@draw ax apps@)tween two sides can feed on itself. To see where it leads, give Harbourtown two payment apps, HarbourPay and QuayPay, equally good. The  (@draw illus@)numbers are illustrative.

Each round, every shopper and shop picks one app, lumped together to  (@draw nread@)keep it simple. They like an app more when more of the town pays with it, plus a dash of personal taste.

HarbourPay  (@draw p0@)start (@draw g0 p1 g1 p2 g2 p3 g3 p4 g4 p5 g5 p6 g6 p7 g7 p8 g8 p9 g9 p10@)s with fifty-five percent of the town's payments. Each dot is its share in the next round.

B: With a weak pull, taste win (@draw endread@)s. The lead melts back, and the town  (@highlight p10 effect circle@)settles at about half each.

Now strengthen the pull, (@highlight nread effect underline@) n: how much an app gains as more of the town joins it.

    quiz "The pull rises from 0.5 to 2. What happens to HarbourPay's five-point lead?" intro "Your guess first." judge false
        right: Watch the dots.
        * It melts back to a split
        * It stays at 55%
        * It snowballs to nearly everyone

The lead snowballs. Fifty-five becomes sixty, then seventy, and within a few rounds nearly the whole town is on HarbourPay.
    animate n 2 duration 3 ghost true

B: Same apps, sam (@highlight p0 effect circle@)e quality. A five-point head start decided everything.

That's tipping (@draw tipword@). Past a certain strength, a split market can't stay split: it falls to one side.

    erase tipword

B: And the middl (@draw tip tip_l@)e is a knife edge.  (@erase p0_ghost p1_ghost p2_ghost p3_ghost p4_ghost p5_ghost p6_ghost p7_ghost p8_ghost p9_ghost p10_ghost g0_ghost g1_ghost g2_ghost g3_ghost g4_ghost g5_ghost g6_ghost g7_ghost g8_ghost g9_ghost@)Start Harbou (@animate s0 45 duration 2.5@)rPay at forty-five instead, and QuayPay takes the town. Half is the tipping point.

Try it: drag the pull n and the starting share. Find the strength where the split stops holding.
    explore params ["n","s0"]

Ju (@animate n 0.9 s0 55 duration 1.5@)st below one, it drifts back; j (@animate n 1.2 duration 1.5 ghost true@)ust above, it runs away. One is the knife's edge, where a lead barely moves.

    pause 0.4

    erase p0_ghost_2 p1_ghost_2 p2_ghost_2 p3_ghost_2 p4_ghost_2 p5_ghost_2 p6_ghost_2 p7_ghost_2 p8_ghost_2 p9_ghost_2 p10_ghost_2 g0_ghost_2 g1_ghost_2 g2_ghost_2 g3_ghost_2 g4_ghost_2 g5_ghost_2 g6_ghost_2 g7_ghost_2 g8_ghost_2 g9_ghost_2

    fade ax p0 p1 p2 p3 p4 p5 p6 p7 p8 p9 p10 g0 g1 g2 g3 g4 g5 g6 g7 g8 g9 tip tip_l apps illus nread endread to 0.15

    draw mathq

    ask "Show the math, or skip it?" on mathq stage own voice b
        intro: Want the rule behind the dots?

@math

    erase mathq

Each round, HarbourPay's new share is one over one plus e to the minus two n, times two times the old share minus one.
    draw mcard_box mcard_line_1

At an even split the share is a half, and the next share is a half again: the split is always a resting point.
    draw mcard_line_2

But the slope there is exactly n. Above one, a small lead comes back bigger every round, so the split can't hold.
    draw mcard_line_3

    erase mcard

@test

    erase mathq

    fade ax p0 p1 p2 p3 p4 p5 p6 p7 p8 p9 p10 g0 g1 g2 g3 g4 g5 g6 g7 g8 g9 tip tip_l apps illus nread endread to 1

    pause 0.3

