    text sure1 "Can tip a market:\nMOSTLY AGREED" #2f6b8f x 410 y 560 font_size 40 hidden true
    text sure2 "How strong, how lasting:\nCONTESTED" #b5482e x 410 y 420 font_size 40 hidden true
    text zhu "Zhu & Iansiti 2019: multi-homing, local clusters" #8f887c cites ["zhu2019"] x 410 y 330 font_size 26 hidden true
    cards sortc check each hidden true
        Speeds tipping: Messages stay in one app, Every user helps all, Two apps a hassle
        Slows tipping: Users keep two apps, Shops take every card, Tastes differ
    text fin "Each side brings the other:\nsmall lead → almost everything\n…unless people keep a second app" #3d3833 x 500 y 420 font_size 44 hidden true

    card title "The brake"

So a st (@draw ax p0 p1 p2 p3 p4 p5 p6 p7 p8 p9 p10 g0 g1 g2 g3 g4 g5 g6 g7 g8 g9 tip tip_l nread endread@)rong pull and a head start hand one platform the whole town. But that rests on a quiet assumption: everyone uses only one app.

B: Real people don't. I've got (@highlight p10 endread@) two ride apps on my phone, and many drivers run both at once.

That's multi-homing, the escape that keeps a split (@draw mread@) platform's network whole. Say sixty percent of Harbourtown keeps both apps: will HarbourPay still take everything?

    quiz "Sixty percent of the town keeps both apps. Does HarbourPay still take everything?" intro "Your call." judge false
        right: Watch the dots.
        * Yes, it still tips
        * No, the split survives

The spl (@animate m 60 duration 3 ghost true@)it survives. When most people can reach everyone anyway, the bigger app's pull fades, and the leader can't starve its rival.

B: That's American ride-hailing. In (@draw uber@) March 2024 Uber took about three-quarters of rideshare spending: the market leans to Uber but doesn't tip, and Lyft lives on.

    erase uber

And cards: most sho (@draw cards@)ps take both Visa and Mastercard, so neither network can shut the other out.

    erase cards

B: Tipping isn't forever, either. My (@draw myspace@)Space led social networking until Facebook passed it worldwide in April 2008.

    erase myspace

How s (@fade ax p0 p1 p2 p3 p4 p5 p6 p7 p8 p9 p10 g0 g1 g2 g3 g4 g5 g6 g7 g8 g9 tip tip_l p0_ghost p1_ghost p2_ghost p3_ghost p4_ghost p5_ghost p6_ghost p7_ghost p8_ghost p9_ghost p10_ghost g0_ghost g1_ghost g2_ghost g3_ghost g4_ghost g5_ghost g6_ghost g7_ghost g8_ghost g9_ghost to 0.15@)ure are we? That cross-side network ef (@draw sure1@)fects can tip a market to one winner: mostly agreed.

B: How strong they are in  (@draw sure2@)a real market, and how long a lead lasts: contested.

Feng Zhu and (@draw zhu@) Marco Iansiti argue it turns on multi-homing, and on whether the network is one global web or many local ones.

    erase sure1 sure2 zhu

    fade ax p0 p1 p2 p3 p4 p5 p6 p7 p8 p9 p10 g0 g1 g2 g3 g4 g5 g6 g7 g8 g9 tip tip_l p0_ghost p1_ghost p2_ghost p3_ghost p4_ghost p5_ghost p6_ghost p7_ghost p8_ghost p9_ghost p10_ghost g0_ghost g1_ghost g2_ghost g3_ghost g4_ghost g5_ghost g6_ghost g7_ghost g8_ghost g9_ghost to 1

    quiz "Which market is most likely to tip to a single winner?" intro "Three new cases."
        right: The messaging app: every user adds to everyone's value, and running two is a hassle.
        wrong: Tipping needs a strong pull and few people using two at once: here, the messaging app.
        * Ride-hailing in one city, where drivers run two apps
        + A messaging app that can only reach people on the same app
        * Petrol stations along a motorway

    hide ax p0 p1 p2 p3 p4 p5 p6 p7 p8 p9 p10 g0 g1 g2 g3 g4 g5 g6 g7 g8 g9 tip tip_l p0_ghost p1_ghost p2_ghost p3_ghost p4_ghost p5_ghost p6_ghost p7_ghost p8_ghost p9_ghost p10_ghost g0_ghost g1_ghost g2_ghost g3_ghost g4_ghost g5_ghost g6_ghost g7_ghost g8_ghost g9_ghost nread mread endread

    draw sortc

    ask "Which of these speed tipping up, and which slow it down? Sort each card." on sortc store f stage own voice b
        right: All six. A strong pull and costly switching speed it; second apps and varied tastes slow it.
        wrong: {f} of {f.total} on the first try: speed it, one-app messages, every user helps all, two apps a hassle; slow it, second apps, every card taken, different tastes.

    erase sortc

    show ax p0 p1 p2 p3 p4 p5 p6 p7 p8 p9 p10 g0 g1 g2 g3 g4 g5 g6 g7 g8 g9 tip tip_l p0_ghost p1_ghost p2_ghost p3_ghost p4_ghost p5_ghost p6_ghost p7_ghost p8_ghost p9_ghost p10_ghost g0_ghost g1_ghost g2_ghost g3_ghost g4_ghost g5_ghost g6_ghost g7_ghost g8_ghost g9_ghost nread mread endread

    quiz "A new Harbourtown app links boat owners with tourists who want to rent a boat. Which launch is most likely to get past the tipping point?" voice b
        right: Pay the boat owners: one side filled first gives the other a reason to come.
        wrong: A small, spread-out bonus never lifts either side past the tipping point; fill one side first: here, the boat owners.
        * A small discount for everyone, for a year
        + Pay boat owners to list, so tourists find a full app from day one
        * Wait for word of mouth

    erase ax p0 p1 p2 p3 p4 p5 p6 p7 p8 p9 p10 g0 g1 g2 g3 g4 g5 g6 g7 g8 g9 tip tip_l p0_ghost p1_ghost p2_ghost p3_ghost p4_ghost p5_ghost p6_ghost p7_ghost p8_ghost p9_ghost p10_ghost g0_ghost g1_ghost g2_ghost g3_ghost g4_ghost g5_ghost g6_ghost g7_ghost g8_ghost g9_ghost nread mread endread

So why do platforms win so big? Because each side brings th (@draw fin@)e other, a small lead snowballs into almost everything, unless people keep a second app.

B: Which is why breaking such a winner up costs the v (@highlight fin effect underline@)ery network that made it win. And who should pay, when the user pays nothing, is the next puzzle: why is Google free?

    pause 0.4

## Where next
